<section class="three-tool-knowledge" id="events-deep-guide"><h2>Islamic Events Calendar: Ramadan, both Eids, Ashura and Hajj dates</h2><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> The Islamic calendar is lunar. Observances such as Ramadan, Eid al-Fitr, Eid al-Adha and the day of Arafah fall on Hijri dates, but their equivalent Gregorian days vary by year, locality and moon-sighting announcements. This page provides labelled calculated Umm al-Qura civil-date estimates, not confirmed festival declarations.</p><nav class="three-tool-nav" aria-label="Guide sections"><a href="#events-deep-dates">Which major Islamic dates </a><a href="#events-deep-calendar">Why Islamic events move th</a><a href="#events-deep-moon">Umm al-Qura versus local m</a><a href="#events-deep-steps">How to browse and export e</a><a href="#events-deep-example">Worked example</a><a href="#events-deep-sources">Sources</a></nav><div class="three-tool-facts"><div><strong>Calendar: Umm al-Qura</strong></div><div><strong>Major events: seven</strong></div><div><strong>Export: CSV and ICS</strong></div></div><section class="three-tool-section" id="events-deep-dates"><h3>Which major Islamic dates appear in the calendar?</h3><p>The reference index includes Islamic New Year at 1 Muharram, Ashura on 10 Muharram, the beginning of Ramadan on 1 Ramadan, Eid al-Fitr on 1 Shawwal, the beginning of Hajj rites on 8 Dhul Hijjah, the day of Arafah on 9 Dhul Hijjah, and Eid al-Adha on 10 Dhul Hijjah. These are conventional Hijri labels, not confirmed future local civil days.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="events-deep-calendar"><h3>Why Islamic events move through the Gregorian year</h3><p>Twelve lunar months total approximately 354 or 355 days, unlike the longer Gregorian solar year. Islamic observances therefore move earlier through the seasons over successive solar years. Quran 2:189 describes lunar crescents as markers of time, and Quran 9:36 refers to twelve months.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="events-deep-moon"><h3>Umm al-Qura versus local moon sighting</h3><p>The calculator uses the Umm al-Qura system to suggest civil-date equivalents. Actual Ramadan and Eid observances may use local crescent sightings, an official authority's announcement or a different adopted calculation system. The optional day-shift is a transparent illustration, not a forecast or official ruling.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="events-deep-steps"><h3>How to browse and export estimated events</h3><p>Select the relevant Hijri year, filter all events, fasting-related dates, Hajj dates or Eids, and compare the calculated Gregorian dates with the optional illustrative local shift. Export CSV for a spreadsheet or ICS to an external calendar. The calendar export deliberately contains unshifted dates and describes them as estimates.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="events-deep-example"><h3>Worked example: following three Dhul Hijjah dates</h3><p>The conventional Hajj-related sequence is 8 Dhul Hijjah for the beginning of Hajj rites, 9 Dhul Hijjah for Arafah, and 10 Dhul Hijjah for Eid al-Adha. The tool converts each numbered Hijri day to a calculated Gregorian civil date for your chosen year. Official Saudi and local announcements may differ.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="events-deep-sunset"><h3>Why an Islamic event can begin the previous evening</h3><p>An Islamic religious day is reckoned from sunset in the relevant location. A calendar line saying 1 Ramadan on a Thursday civil date does not specify the exact moment Ramadan begins or local Taraweeh timing. Confirm the lunar month's announcement and local prayer timetable.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="events-deep-laylat"><h3>Can the calendar predict Laylatul Qadr?</h3><p>No. Laylatul Qadr is sought especially within the last ten nights of Ramadan, and no universal fixed Gregorian date can be guaranteed for the future. A calendar that labels a particular evening as certainly Laylatul Qadr overstates its knowledge. Use the separate Laylatul Qadr planner for a cautious schedule.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="events-deep-mistakes"><h3>Planning travel, leave and community gatherings responsibly</h3><p>Treat estimates as provisional when booking nonrefundable travel, school absence, mosque events or Eid leave. Recheck official community announcements nearer the date. Exported all-day reminders are civil labels, not precise sunset notifications or religious determinations.</p></section><section class="three-tool-section" id="events-deep-faq"><h3>Frequently asked questions</h3>{faqs.map(([q,a])=><details class="faq-detail"><summary>{q}</summary><p>{a}</p></details>)}</section><section class="three-tool-section" id="events-deep-sources"><h3>Sources and further reading</h3><ul><li><a href="https://quran.com/2/189" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Quran 2:189</a></li><li><a href="https://quran.com/9/36" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Quran 9:36</a></li><li><a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:1909" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih al-Bukhari 1909</a></li></ul><p class="small">Educational reference, not a binding religious ruling. Qualified scholarly review pending.</p></section></section>